<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">South Brunswick, New Jersey</p>
    <h1>Stop the data center at 4405 Route 1</h1>
    <p class="lede">A data center going up next to homes on Route 1 wants a state permit for 12 diesel generators, with no limit on emergency use and no monitoring of what comes out.</p>
    <div class="standing">
      <h2>Where things stand</h2>
      <p>The township approved the building. The generators can't run without a state air permit, and that permit isn't final.</p>
      <p><strong>Thank you to everyone who commented.</strong> About 5,000 comments went to the state before the November 9 deadline. NJDEP must respond to every comment before it decides.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Three things to know</h2>
  <div class="three">
    <div class="point">
      <h3>Power</h3>
      <p>Bloomberg, the tenant, says it will draw up to 30 megawatts, about twice what every home in South Brunswick uses on average. Its backup generators total about 43 megawatts. The developer calls these 12 generators "Phase 1." Its representative told the Planning Board 8 more generators are planned, 20 in all.</p>
      <a class="readmore" href="/south-brunswick/#power">Read more →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="point">
      <h3>Noise</h3>
      <p>Cooling equipment that runs 24/7, plus 12 diesel engines tested during the day. The noise study gives no low-frequency results and never modeled the generators at full power.</p>
      <a class="readmore" href="/south-brunswick/#noise">Read more →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="point">
      <h3>Air</h3>
      <p>Sentinel's health numbers count only scheduled testing, and even then the cancer risk barely passes. Emergencies, when all 12 engines can run for days, aren't counted at all.</p>
      <a class="readmore" href="/south-brunswick/#testing-only">Read more →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

